  "body": "## What changed\n\n- New module `crates/kernel/src/mesh_pole.rs`. It finds a triangle corner that sits on a pole of a spline face. At a pole, the derivative along one parameter is zero.\n- The module moves the free parameter of that corner to the mean of the other two corners. It takes those two corners on one side of the period.\n- The module keeps the move only when the surface point stays within 1e-9 mm.\n- `mesh_deviation::face` calls the module for spline faces before it samples a triangle. Analytic faces do not change.\n- New fixture `testdata/models/sphere_quarter_pole.step`. This is our own file: a quarter sphere, R 25, with a rational B-spline dome that turns 180 degrees and ends in a pole at the apex.\n\n## Cause\n\nThe issue reports an open mesh on v0.24.0. On upstream/main 8be100737 (after #917), the import itself refuses the q02 part:\n\n```\nsolid #3738 'Mould' could not be tessellated: machining mesh: face Some(10) stands 5.089e-1 mm off its surface, tolerance 5e-3 mm\n```\n\nFace 10 is the ellipsoid boss (`#3666`, a rational B-spline with a pole at its top). The mesh is good. Its sag against the ellipsoid is about 0.004 mm. The planner gives the apex node u = 0 (and u = 1), and the ring under it has u near 0.5. The measure interpolated u across half the period in each fan triangle. It then projected from a hint on the far side of the surface. The bound of those triangles became 0.29 mm. The bound did not change at a finer chord. This showed that the measure was wrong and the mesh was not.\n\n## Readings (q02 STEP from the QA host, local only)\n\n- Before: the import refuses with the message above.\n- After: 48780 facets, 0 open edges.\n- After: the volume is 97163.50 mm^3. SolidCraft gives 97165.69 mm^3.\n- After: the slice at z = 19.6875 (the reported height) gives 1382.27 mm^2. Slices at z = 18.5, 20, 25 and 29 also close.\n\n## Tests\n\nPins:\n- `camcraft-kernel` `mesh_deviation_pole::a_fan_at_a_spline_pole_measures_by_its_true_sag`. Red before the fix: \"a good fan at a pole is judged 0.1065280187198439 mm off\".\n- `camcraft-kernel` `mesh_deviation_pole::a_solid_with_a_spline_pole_meshes_inside_the_tolerance`. Red before the fix: \"face Some(2) stands 2.775e-1 mm off its surface, tolerance 5e-3 mm\" (the same refusal as q02).\n- `camcraft-app` `import_step_spline_pole::a_solid_with_a_spline_pole_imports_closed_and_slices`. Red before the fix with the same refusal. Green after: volume and five slices.\n- `camcraft-kernel` lib `mesh_pole::tests` (3 unit tests).\n\nMutation: I put back `mesh_deviation.rs` from upstream/main. The three pins above went red. I restored the fix. The pins went green.\n\nOriginal author gate results:\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`: 90 steps. 89 were green on the first run. The one red step was `kernel suites: step_agreement` (`step_files_agree_or_refuse_by_name`: \"a fixture has no row\"). That test requires a row for each STEP fixture. I added the row for `sphere_quarter_pole.step` (`Truck::NotRecorded`, the form for fixtures after #2790) and its `per_file!` test. The bench reads the file as \"watertight, within tolerance\", V = 16358.3 mm^3 (exact pi R^3 / 3 = 16362.5). After that, `step_agreement` is green: 27 passed.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-kernel -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\n## Review follow-up\n\nHead: `bb6480b6554f66a31c0158fbf1a80a9adc81cfa3` (unchanged).\nBase: `upstream/main` at `60cb97a0b3717bf6a129e781fd8f4ec98806124b`.\nNo source files changed during this follow-up.\n\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`: exit 0. All 90 steps passed in 24.0 minutes. Tests: 2212 passed, 0 failed, 31 ignored.\n- `cargo xtask gate heavy --base upstream/main`: exit 0. All 252 reached steps completed in 43.4 minutes. No unexpected step failed.\n- Heavy tests: 104 passed, 6 known expected failures, 0 ignored. Kernel: 4 passed, 0 failed. Motion: 57 passed, 2 expected failures. App: 43 passed, 4 expected failures.\n- `cargo clippy -j 8 -p camcraft-kernel -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`: exit 0 in 1 minute 18 seconds.\n\nThe six expected failures are tracked by #1950 and #2849:\n\n| Crate | Test | Tracked issue |\n|---|---|---|\n| motion | `ring_tests::the_stock_ring_level_reaches_the_air_planner_with_the_stock` | #1950 |\n| motion | `ring_tests::the_stock_ring_level_plans_adaptive_with_no_budget_note` | #1950 |\n| app | `pocket3d_hsm::the_users_whole_model_bore_roughs_adaptive_on_each_level` | #1950 |\n| app | `lead_approach::an_axis_aligned_lead_start_posts_the_users_arc_entry_as_g18_or_g19` | #2849 |\n| app | `lead_approach::the_horizontal_arc_entry_on_the_users_pocket_verifies_silent` | #2849 |\n| app | `shoulder_adaptive::fig8_outside_containment_with_em0800_and_clearing_kinds_works` | #2849 |\n\nThe gate accepts these known failures. They are not passing tests.\nThe gate used merge-base `8be1007` against the recorded base.\nThe worktree and the goldens did not change.\nNo new pin or mutation check was made. Review finding 1 remains blocked as recorded below.\n\n## Goldens moved\n\nNone.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- Review finding 1 remains open. The recorded q02 STEP is not in the tree. Issue #3034 names only `len:/home/mikekinney/qa/runs/20261120-q02-3d-mold/q02-3d-mold.step`. No local copy, attachment, or alternate source was found. This host cannot resolve `len`. The required recorded-file regression and its mutation check could not run. The existing quarter-sphere fixture does not complete this finding.\n- I did not run a Waterline, 3D Pocket, Scallop or Pencil generate on q02. The slices that those operations use close at the reported height.\n- The 1e-9 mm point check in `settle` has no separate pin. No surface that we can build has a zero derivative at a point that is not a pole.\n\n## Overlaps\n\nNone. No open PR changes `crates/kernel/`.\n\nChangelog: A closed STEP solid with a spline face that ends in a pole (for example an ellipsoid or a dome from SolidCraft) now imports. Before, the import refused it with \"stands ... mm off its surface\".\n\nFixes #3034\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
